#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152055 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ffdfb29-a3d2-5a7a-868c-21eab8965922" :knowledge-point/title "Modular Residues of Positive Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "c165bfd1-f6cc-5849-b519-f3763e481cbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-14583" :question/problem "What is $26 \\textrm{mod} 3$?" :question/worked-solution "The residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r < 3\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n26 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r$, we need to write $26$ as an integer multiple of $3$, plus the residue $r$.\n\nWe note that $26 \\equiv 2 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$ because\n\n$$\n26 = 8\\cdot 3 + {\\color{blue}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property. Remember that any integer multiple of $3$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $3$.\n\n$$\n26 \\mid ≡26 - 0\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡26 - 8 \\cdot 3\\end{vmatrix}(mod3); ≡26 - 24\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡2\\end{vmatrix}(mod3)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152056 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70cef00-bf25-5423-85ac-2d3a44c68f35" :knowledge-point/title "Modular Residues of Negative Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "6f664a47-49c5-5183-bdcc-42ba86553a67" :question/math-academy-id "e-14584" :question/problem "What is $-13 \\textrm{mod} 4$?" :question/worked-solution "The residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r < 4\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n-13 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r$, we need to write $-13$ as an integer multiple of $4$, plus the residue $r$.\n\nWe note that $-13 \\equiv 3 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n-13 = (-4)\\cdot 4 + {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property. Remember that any integer multiple of $4$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $4$.\n\n$$\n-13 \\mid ≡ - 13 + 0\\begin{vmatrix}(mod4) \\\\ ≡ - 13 + 4 \\cdot 4\\end{vmatrix}(mod4); ≡ - 13 + 16\\begin{vmatrix}(mod4) \\\\ ≡3\\end{vmatrix}(mod4)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151857 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152057 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a090bd2-cdb1-5680-81bc-3cb36de126c4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Last Few Digits of a Number"}] :question/id #uuid "895dcb35-e68c-5b67-8f8a-8bc895bbde8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14611" :question/problem "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nb=24a+15\n$$\n\nNote that $b$ is positive. Now, let $d_3$, $d_2$, and $d_1$ be the last three digits of the number $b{:}$\n\n$$\nb = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= ⋯ + 100d_{3} + 10d_{2} + d_{1} \\\\\n&= 10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\nb \\mid ≡10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡0 \\cdot (⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡0 + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nNotice that $d_1$ is the last digit of $b$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nb = 24a+15\n$$\n\nand\n$24\\equiv 4, \\qquad 15\\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,10)$\nwe have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}b & ≡d_{1} & (mod10) \\\\ 24a + 15 & ≡d_{1} & (mod10) \\\\ 4a + 5 & ≡d_{1} & (mod10)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(4a+5) \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152057 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a090bd2-cdb1-5680-81bc-3cb36de126c4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Last Few Digits of a Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f168564-63c2-54eb-9d66-e87c51ad8d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149406} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b07ec5-5177-504e-aa5c-fa86bb52fbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149407} {:answer/id #uuid "445812a4-4279-58a4-8fc8-b1d50b6362b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149408} {:answer/id #uuid "4509f76f-a4ee-5ec9-9519-d588feb4ba1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149409}] :answer-field/id #uuid "775341ff-b38e-5bee-af28-a23d72def431" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6828380-01ac-5a3c-855f-7a4e71d4487a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\div" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149411} {:answer/id #uuid "b7afaee0-d8a0-54dd-a041-2dc7bf61e8ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cdot" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149412} {:answer/id #uuid "310dd514-af1c-5159-a13d-b3cc2654b52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "mod" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149413}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2ef34e-9422-58a1-9455-6b2d57dcd007" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149410}] :question/id #uuid "94462753-c983-5b18-8533-8c7f9bc5c0ee" :question/mat